# Splitting a black felt-tip: the spot that is really three
#
# A black ink looks like one colour. Put a dot of it near the bottom of a
# strip of paper, stand the paper in water, and the water climbing past the
# dot carries each dye a different distance — because each one divides
# differently between the water that moves and the water the paper holds.
# The number that says how far is Rf, and it is the same partition
# coefficient that decides how long each dye takes on a column.
register lv1

add v1 water 30mL
add v1 black_ink 2mL
look v1
chromatograph v1

# The dyes on their own, for comparison. Each runs to its own height, and
# the heights are the same ones the mixture just produced — which is how a
# strip identifies what is in an unknown ink.
new beaker
add v2 water 30mL
add v2 food_colour_blue 1mL
chromatograph v2

new beaker
add v3 water 30mL
add v3 food_colour_yellow 1mL
chromatograph v3

# The same numbers, read as a column instead of a strip. One separation,
# two instruments, one partition coefficient: the dye the column holds
# longest is the one nearest the pencil line.
register lv3
chromatograph v1

# What this does not model: the Rf values are reviewed teaching numbers
# rather than measurements, chosen to reproduce which dye runs furthest and
# roughly how far apart the spots land — a real strip's Rf depends on the
# solvent, the paper and the temperature, and none of those are claimed
# here. Dissolved ions travel with the water and are named as outside the
# method rather than quietly dropped.
